    <Header />

    
<main>
  <h1>
    Disassembly and Memory
  </h1>

  <p>
    On the grounds of my grandparents' home in Fujisaki, Aomori,
    stood an agricultural storage shed that had remained unused
    for many years. I dismantled the shed by hand and
    reassembled its salvaged materials into a 26-faced sculpture
    titled <em>Texture Displaced</em>.
  </p>

  <p>
    The sculpture draws inspiration from the menhir, a single
    standing stone erected in prehistoric times. Taking the
    menhir as a symbol of architecture's fundamental verticality,
    the work transforms the history and traces of the former
    structure into a symbolic "reconstructed ruin."
  </p>

  <p>
    The sculpture is a 26-faced polyhedron composed of
    equilateral triangles and squares, each with sides measuring
    1,200 mm. Its surfaces retain the original materials of the
    shed, including weathered wood and metal sheets. Even
    identical materials have aged differently depending on their
    exposure to sunlight, wind, and rain. Each face thus carries
    traces of a distinct passage of time.
  </p>

  <p>
    The work's formal concept is rooted in the relationship
    between "model" and "material" in 3D computer graphics.
    By translating the digital process of treating form and
    surface texture as independent elements into physical
    architectural materials, the work reexamines the relationship
    between memories embedded in materials and the new forms
    they inhabit.
  </p>

  <p>
    Exhibition
  </p>

  <p>
    Solo Exhibition: Disassembly and Memory — Landscapes After Us
    <br />
    November 29 – December 7, 2025
    <br />
    Aomori Museum of Art, Community Galleries A/B
  </p>

  <p>
    Support
  </p>

  <p>
    Agency for Cultural Affairs, Japan
    <br />
    Media Arts Creator Development Support Program 2025
  </p>

  <p>
    Photo Credits
  </p>

  <p>
    Exhibition Photography: Hayate Yagi
    <br />
    Process Photography: Go Izumita
  </p>


